how exactly does oda do all that surely  it must all be planned out down to the  very last detail  or is oda simply a freestyling genius  who prefers to improvise everything with  miraculous consistency to answer this  question i wanted no i  need it to find out more about oda as a  person  and as a storyteller which is  surprisingly hard because as you guys  might know the man likes to stay on the  sidelines preferring to let us work  speak for itself there wasn't a lot of  articles magazines or even interviews of  oda to go with  but i had to start somewhere as i poured  through the little material that i had  the first thing i came to realize is  that oda or oda sensei as most people in  japan like to call him respectfully  is actually a little bit dishonest when  it comes to talking about his work for  instance in one interview oda stated  that he doesn't really try to put any  deeper meanings into a story however  this is clearly not the case as we all  know that one piece is notorious for its  underlying themes and political and  moral problems in its narrative  the racism towards the fishman the  injustice of the world government  the power of dreams  freedom  you get what i mean now in another  interview oda stated that he absolutely  hates tearjerkers and storytelling  however oda himself admits that one  piece doesn't exactly suffer from a  shortage of heartbreaking moments and in  general oda likes to mention that he  simply draws what he feels like drawing  and often just improvises as he moves  along  a famous example of this is for example  the introduction of the supernova as i  found out through my digging however  this is also not entirely true because  as many of those working with him have  commented oda is an absolute  perfectionist when it comes to his work  and oda himself knows and acknowledges  this his obsession with telling a  perfect story actually comes up quite  frequently in his interviews here when  asked what about his work makes him the  most happy his answer was  that probably have to be whenever i make  a good story i'm a total wreck when i  can't do my job well i wind up mopping  if i don't feel like i've fully  completed a good story i can't rest  until i make up for it the next week in  writing one piece oda gets his  inspiration from history folklore and  especially from film  his favorite directors include the likes  of tim burton hayao miyazaki and quentin  tarantino  and what all of these directors have in  common is their desire to tell the  perfect story  so  improvisation  and perfectionism can these two things  actually go together i wondered and even  if oda somehow did figure out that a  long-running story can keep delivering  the question again is  how  i had to dig deeper first off i've  always kind of wondered how oda had the  time and of course also the energy to  come up with random fun facts about one  piece characters and share them in an  spss or on some other platform looking  through more of the interviews i  stumbled across one mind-blowing  technique that oda uses not only to keep  the story full of energy and interesting  but surely must be the foundation for  his crazy web of characters  all characters in one piece are designed  to be real people  let me explain when writing his story  oda seems to put himself into the shoes  of each of his characters as you might  for example do when trying to understand  another person's perspective in the real  world  in this way he's able to discern what a  character would realistically do in any  given moment  basically next level empathy so  how does he do that well have you ever  wondered what these really weird details  are that we often learn about characters  in manga  like what is doflamingo's favorite food  what is zorro's blood type or  how tall is law oh and by the way  when is kaido's birthday as hirohiko  araki the author of jojo explains in his  book  it's apparently considered best practice  among manga cars to create crazily  detailed profiles of each major  character  everything from basic physical  attributes over their past histories  down to specific personality traits and  quirks now this may seem like an  unnecessary amount of time to spend  thinking about each individual character  especially since many of these facts  don't even come up in the story in the  first place  but that's precisely the point getting  to know your characters so well and in  so much detail that you can practically  predict what they would do next  this is  how oda writes  that's why one piece characters rally  or dare i even say never feel out of  character but oda takes this even  further i write in pretty menu detail  when someone makes a certain action  how people around that individual will  respond normally when making a story  move along it's fine to simply draw the  main characters but rather than that i  want to write about what the townspeople  are thinking by putting all feelings in  their place within a scene  this is what we're dealing with here  folks oda basically lets his characters  perform like actors on a stage and  ultimately it's this method of organic  writing that leads to the story  inevitably sidetracking and expanding on  its own  having created his characters in such  rich detail they pretty much advance the  plot for oda  this is next level writing there is one  big problem with this kind of writing  technique though  it's very  very easy to lose focus and have the  story move further and further away from  the original plot i'm sure you've seen  stories like this before where suddenly  the sight plot becomes the main plot and  readers are left totally confused as to  what actually happened to the original  story however oda seems to do this so  flawlessly  that every single plot actually seems to  contribute to the oval story arc he  himself said that the only things that  he was absolutely sure of before even  starting one piece were luffy his powers  the ending and that he wanted to draw  one heck of an epic adventure pretty  much the exact opposite of having  everything and everyone planned out  right from the start  however these specific elements are  actually just what any good story needs  before you start writing they basically  act as a sort of framework with a set of  rules to operate in  oda knew who luffy is and exactly what  he wanted right from the start  he also knew what the story would be  about and what would mark its beginning  and ending this way no matter how his  characters would behave or move  throughout the story  they would always be striving toward  that ending giving the story structure  and direction despite this improvised  style  and even further than that establishing  the rules of the world right from the  start allows for consistent and organic  plots there are devil fruits with  special powers in this world it's a  world of pirates mostly covered by water  monsters exist and anything can happen  on the grand line  oda establishes these things right from  the start and within these rules can let  his characters run completely free this  exceptionally strong set of rules and  the overall direction of the plot that  oda has established ultimately allow him  to let his characters move freely and  the plot take him wherever it leads him  and so it finally clicked for me  the secret to oda's genius is that he's  able to flawlessly combine planning and  improvisation to create an entire new  world of thousands of individual  characters and hundreds of locations all  based on his inspiration  and imagination a story that rightfully  has breached 1 000 chapters and that  boasts having set the world record for  most copies published for the same comic  book by the same author it clearly is  the best selling mod in history for a  reason now if you were just as impressed  by this as i am  let me tell you that you can copy this  approach as well to reach your own goals  in the future
 

